// Memory a global still refers to at the end isn't a leak; memory nothing
// refers to any more is.
let keep = alloc(10)
let msg = ask(capture(), "hi")
fn scratch() {
    let tmp = alloc(4)
    return 1
}
scratch()
// expect: [sim    150ms] camera: photo #1 captured (22 bytes)
// expect: [sim    150ms] AI: sending photo + "hi"
// expect: [sim   1950ms] AI: answered
// expect: leak: buf #1 (22 bytes) allocated at line 4 was never freed, and nothing refers to it any more
// expect: leak: buf #3 (4 bytes) allocated at line 6 was never freed, and nothing refers to it any more
// expect: hint: free() memory from alloc(), arrays and built strings once you're done with it. Memory a global variable still holds at the end is fine, but on a device that runs for days, a leak eventually uses up all memory.
